  "narrative": {
    "intro": "South Carolina recorded $521M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date. The state saw 511 awards, with an average award size of $1M, and obligations fell 94.8% year over year. Federal buying this fiscal year so far is concentrated in a few large awards and a small number of agencies. The Department of Energy is the single largest buyer, obligating $421M, and Savannah River contractors are the top recipients. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligations, accounting for $380M. Historical context shows larger full-year totals in prior years: FY22 totaled $7.2B, FY23 totaled $10.2B, FY24 totaled $10.7B, FY25 totaled $8.4B, and FY26 totaled $10.1B. Current-year totals are partial, and the FY2027 figure reflects fiscal year to date activity. Companies and agencies tracking South Carolina opportunities should note the heavy concentration around energy cleanup and site support work and the difference between this partial-year snapshot and the multi-billion-dollar full years that preceded it.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the change in federal contracting this year?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy obligated $421M, concentrating a large share of this fiscal year’s obligations. Obligations fell 94.8% year over year, reflecting that FY2027 to date is a partial total compared with prior full fiscal years. Historical full-year obligations include $7.2B in FY22, $10.2B in FY23, $10.7B in FY24, $8.4B in FY25, and $10.1B in FY26."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate South Carolina federal contracts?",
        "body": "Facilities Support Services is the largest industry, with $380M in obligations. SAVANNAH RIVER NUCLEAR SOLUTIONS LLC is the largest vendor, receiving $379M, and other top vendors include BATTELLE SAVANNAH RIVER ALLIANCE, LLC with $25.5M and FEDERAL EXPRESS CORPORATION with $25.1M. Major industry categories also include Couriers and Express Delivery Services at $25.9M and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $26.5M."
      }
    ]
  },
